WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.370
MINHAZ KAZI: Welcome
to DataVis DevTalk.

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:04.680
I'm your host, Minhaz
Kazi, developer advocate

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:06.300
for Data Studio.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.340
In this episode for
DataVis DevTalk,

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:11.010
you'll learn how authentication
flow works for community

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:14.160
connectors and how you
can authorize your users

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.079
to use external services.

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:17.356
Let's get started.

00:00:17.356 --> 00:00:20.618
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:26.100
When a user tries to use a
connector for the first time,

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:29.310
Data Studio triggers the
Connector Authorization flow.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:32.200
Connector Authorization
is a 2-step process.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:35.100
First, the Google
OAuth2 flow is triggered

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.260
to allow Data Studio to
execute the connector.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:40.530
This flow includes all
the necessary scopes

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:42.300
required by the connector.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:45.420
We talked about the OAuth scopes
in our episode about Manifest.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:48.150
In the first step, the
user will review the scopes

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:50.580
and complete the
authorization process.

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:54.270
In the second step, Data Studio
triggers additional OAuth flow

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:57.720
if the connector needs to
reach any external service.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.390
This second step is
initiated by Data Studio

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:04.260
by making the getAuthType
call to the connector.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.270
At this point, there
are several use cases

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:08.490
when you will not need
any additional credentials

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:10.050
from the user.

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:12.630
The first use case is
if you're not connecting

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:14.310
to any external service.

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:16.890
For example, if you're
connecting to the Google Fit

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:21.240
API, the OAuth scope will be a
part of the Google OAuth2 flow

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:24.278
and no further
authorization is required.

00:01:24.278 --> 00:01:26.070
The second use case is
if you're connecting

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:29.430
to an external service that
does not require the user

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:30.840
to authenticate.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.960
For example, if you're fetching
data from the npm Download

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.270
Count public API,
it does not require

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:38.610
any kind of authentication.

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:40.230
And the last use
case is if you're

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:43.830
accessing external services
using a private secret,

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:47.910
like a service account
credential or a token.

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:51.660
For example, the Stack Overflow
API requires an API key.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:53.940
You can generate
your own API key,

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:55.890
store it in your
Script Properties,

00:01:55.890 --> 00:02:00.570
and use the stored API key
every API call you make.

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:03.810
In these use cases, you will
return the Auth type NONE

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:06.030
for the getAuthType response.

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:08.070
Data Studio will then
proceed with the connector

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:10.210
configuration.

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:12.720
However, if you require
a user's credentials

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:15.030
to authenticate to
external services,

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:16.920
you should return the
Auth type required

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:19.350
by your specific
external service.

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:21.630
Your options for
authentication include

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:27.060
API key, username and password,
username and token, and OAuth2.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:29.320
For a complete list of
authentication types,

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:31.320
check out the reference
link for the getAuthType

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:33.797
in the description below.

00:02:33.797 --> 00:02:35.880
If the service you connecting
to requires the user

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:39.480
to provide their credentials
via an OAuth2 process,

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:43.470
you should use the OAuth2
for Apps Script library.

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:46.230
This will make the
implementation a lot easier.

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.090
The library will also
be useful if you're

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:51.090
using service accounts with
an OAuth2-based service.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:53.220
In this type of
authentication, Data Studio

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:55.920
will open a new window
and redirect the user

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.450
to the service
provider's login screen.

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:00.330
Once the user logs
in and provides

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:02.460
the necessary permissions
to the connector,

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:05.280
the user will be able to resume
the connector configuration

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.020
in Data Studio.

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:09.480
If you're using the OAuth2
for Apps Script library

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:11.100
in your connector
code, you'll be

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:14.720
able to access the user
token through the library.

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:15.930
An advanced tip.

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:18.510
If your connector is
connecting to your own service,

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:20.250
you have the
flexibility to set up

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:21.990
any kind of custom
authentication

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:26.940
and authorization flow at your
end using the OAuth2 method.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:29.580
If your connector requires
a different authentication

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:33.150
than OAuth2, like API
key or username-password,

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:35.940
Data Studio will render
the required interface

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:39.532
so the user can provide the
credentials in Data Studio.

00:03:39.532 --> 00:03:41.490
You should use the Apps
Script Property service

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:44.850
and store these credentials
in the User Property Store

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:47.260
and fetch them when required.

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:49.290
You'll need to write a
few additional functions

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:53.130
in your code if you're using
any Auth type other than NONE.

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:54.830
These functions will
allow Data Studio

00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:58.860
to verify the credentials
and reset them when required.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:02.040
Details about these functions,
how to implement them,

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:04.860
along with code examples are
available in the Authentication

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:07.920
Guide linked in the
description below.

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:10.750
To learn more, check out the
links in the description below,

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:13.020
and don't forget to
subscribe to the GCP channel

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:15.217
for more great content
on Data Studio.

00:04:15.217 --> 00:04:16.050
Thanks for watching.

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:18.660
And until next time, keep
building awesome dashboards.

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:22.010
[MUSIC PLAYING]

